<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hryhorii Skovoroda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1722–1794</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Grigory Skovoroda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hryhorii Skovoroda (1722–1794) was a Ukrainian philosopher, poet and teacher, educated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv-mohyla-academy">Kyiv-Mohyla Academy</a>. After teaching at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv-collegium">Kharkiv Collegium</a> he spent his last decades as a wandering thinker in Sloboda <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, writing philosophical dialogues, fables and the verse collection Garden of Divine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> on self-knowledge and work suited to one's nature. He is regarded as a founding figure of Ukrainian philosophy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hryhorii-skovoroda.jpg" alt="Hryhorii Skovoroda" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: portrait by unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hryhoriy_Skovoroda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
